  "worked_solution": "First, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\frac{9{b}^{4}}{16}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{9{b}^{4}}}{\\sqrt{16}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{9{b}^{4}}}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{9{b}^{4}}}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{9{b}^{4}}}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{(3{b}^{2})^{2}}}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{|3{b}^{2}|}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{|3|\\cdot |{b}^{2}|}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\cdot |{b}^{2}|}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\cdot |{b}^{2}|}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\cdot {b}^{2}}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{3{b}^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
